he forEach due to some parsing quirk with the array literal starting a line? Like ASI (automatic semicolon insertion)? NO WAIT — ASI! Here's the thing:\n\n```js\nconst ICON={};\n[['Backspace','3:3'],...].forEach(...)\n```\n\nIf the transpiler or runtime treats `const ICON={}` + newline + `[...]` — ASI doesn't insert a semicolon after `}` of an object literal?? Actually ASI: `const ICON={}\\n[...]` — the parser sees `const ICON={}[...]` — no wait. `const ICON = {} ['Backspace','3:3']...` — THIS IS IT! `{}[...]` — member access on the object literal! `const ICON={}\\n[['Backspace','3:3'],...]` parses as `const ICON = {}[['Backspace','3:3'],...].forEach(...)` — the array literal is a MEMBER ACCESS expression on `{}`?? No wait — `{}['Backspace']` is member access with string literal → undefined. And then `,['Card','3:7']...` — comma operator inside brackets: `{}[['Backspace','3:3'],['Card','3:7'],...].forEach(...)` — the bracket contains a comma-separated sequence of arrays... Actually `[['a','b'],['c','d']].forEach` — inside brackets, it's `['a','b'], ['c','d'], ...` which is the comma operator evaluating to the last element `['TransferIn','3:24']`... then `['TransferIn','3:24'].forEach(...)` — array forEach — wait no.\n\n`{}[['Backspace','3:3'],['Card','3:7'],['Location','3:13'],['Transfer','3:23'],['TransferIn','3:24']].forEach(([k,id])=>ICON[k]=figma.getNodeById(id))`\n\nParse: `{} [ X ].forEach(f)` where X = `['Backspace','3:3'],['Card','3:7'],...,['TransferIn','3:24']` (comma expression) → evaluates to last: `['TransferIn','3:24']`. Then `['TransferIn','3:24'].forEach(...)` — iterating over ['TransferIn','3:24'] with callback ([k,id])=>... hmm, forEach over 2-element array: callback called with ('TransferIn', 0, arr) and ('3:24', 1, arr). Destructured [k,id] = ['TransferIn', 0]?? No — callback receives (element, index, array). First call element='TransferIn', index=0 → k='TransferIn', id=0 → ICON['TransferIn']=figma.getNodeById(0)=null. Second: k='3:24', id=1 → ICON['3:24']=null.\n\nSo ICON ends up with only garbage keys: TransferIn (null), '3:24' (null). ICON['Card'] = undefined. 💥 EXACTLY matches the error!\n\nASI did NOT insert a semicolon because `{}` followed by `[` is valid continuation (member access). Classic JS pitfall. My diagnostic call didn't have this pattern (I used a for...of over const array on separate lines with proper structure — actually I used `for (const [k,id] of [...])` on ONE statement so no ASI issue).\n\nGREAT. Root cause found: missing semicolon + line-leading bracket. Fix: use object literal assignment for ICON (no forEach), which sidesteps everything.\n\nNow, rewrite the full component build call with:\n1. ICON/LOGO as object literals.\n2. setBtnLabel WITHOUT t.y (no x/y on instance children). Given Button used at native size on screens — label override only (characters, textAutoResize NONE, align CENTER).\n3. Dialog: Cancel = VI/Button Secondary (137×52), Buy = VI/Button Compact (137×52) — my own masters.\n4. Button Secondary/Compact as described.\n\nAlso fix buildQuickAction's recolor on icon instance — that worked before in Tab Bar build (per summary \"Icon recoloring: recursive recolor ... of icon instances\" was used). OK.\n\nOne more thing: in buildQuickAction I do `comp.appendChild(circ); circ.x=27; circ.y=0;` — that's a child of a COMPONENT (not instance), fine.\n\nPinCell Empty: strokes solid gray — solid() returns array, ok. dashPattern on component fine.\n\nAccountOption Selected: two ellipses — inner positioned x339,y20+... fine (component children can be positioned freely).\n\nAlso chipSet label 'Day' — for other chips (Week/Month/Year/All) I'll override characters on instances; label is fixed-width? I made lb with auto-resize (mkText default autoResize WIDTH_AND_HEIGHT) centered by x=(64-w)/2. On instance, overriding characters changes width → center shifts! Must make chip label a fixed-width centered box: textAutoResize='NONE', resize(64,18), x=0, align CENTER. Same for KeypadKey 